# FieldX

GPU-first Bayesian refinement of crystallographic electron density beyond the local atomistic parameter manifold.

The first target is **PDB 6O2H**, ambient-temperature triclinic lysozyme at 1.21 A. The deposited model has approximately `Rwork=0.097` and `Rfree=0.117`. v1 asks whether a continuous, experimentally band-limited density correction can lower **both** values without using the free reflections for fitting or model selection.

## Scientific model

The inferred mean density is

\[
\rho(\mathbf r)=\rho_0(\mathbf r)+L_\theta z,
\]

where `rho0` is generated from the atomic model with Gemmi `DensityCalculatorX` and `L_theta` is a stationary band-limited prior operator. The repository compares:

- Matérn fields;
- squared-exponential fields;
- band-limited white fields as a control;
- multiscale Matérn mixtures;
- Gaussian, Student-t, smoothed-Laplace and Cauchy latent statistics;
- Gaussian and Student-t observation losses.

The model density is intentionally **unblurred**. Gemmi scaling and bulk-solvent calibration are handled separately so FFT acceleration tricks are not confused with the physical field.

## Validation barrier

Reflections have three roles:

1. `train`: field fitting;
2. `tune`: internal prior selection and early stopping, drawn from the original work set;
3. `free`: deposited R-free set, excluded from fitting and selection.

During prior comparison, Gemmi anisotropic/bulk-solvent scaling is fitted on `train` only. After the prior is frozen, scaling is refitted on the full work set and the field is refit on the full work set. A SHA-256 `MODEL_LOCK.json` is required before `evaluate-free` can run.

The one-shot report contains

\[
\Delta R_{work},\quad \Delta R_{free},\quad \Delta(R_{free}-R_{work}).
\]

## 6O2H data layout

```text
data/6O2H/6O2H.mtz
data/6O2H/6O2H.cif
data/6O2H/triclinic_lysozyme_maps.h5
```

The HDF5 file is only inspected in v1; diffuse scattering belongs to v2.

The default MTZ labels are `FP`, `SIGFP`, `FREE`, with free flag value `0`. Verify them before production:

```bash
uv run fieldrefine inspect configs/6o2h/default.yaml
```

## uv environment

The project uses one `pyproject.toml` and a `uv` project environment. On the first networked machine, create `uv.lock` once and commit it:

```bash
uv lock --python 3.11
```

For CPU development:

```bash
uv sync --locked --extra cpu --group dev
make check
```

For a CUDA cluster:

```bash
scripts/bootstrap_cluster.sh cuda13 "$SCRATCH/cfi-uv"
source environment/cluster.env
scripts/cluster_preflight.sh
```

Use `cuda12` instead if required by the site. The bootstrap creates the lockfile first if the transferred repository does not yet contain one, then performs a locked sync. Compute jobs use `uv run --frozen --no-sync` and never resolve dependencies.

## SLURM pipeline

Set site-specific options without editing job files:

```bash
export CFI_SBATCH_ARGS="--account=MYACCOUNT --partition=MYGPU"
export CFI_ARRAY_LIMIT=8
scripts/submit.sh
```

The dependency graph is:

```text
inspect
  -> prepare reflections/splits
  -> Gemmi rho0 + solvent mask
  -> train-only Gemmi scaling
  -> GPU FFT + derivative checks
  -> GPU pytest gate
  -> atomic xyz/B/occupancy representability benchmark
  -> parallel prior sweep on train/tune
  -> select prior
  -> work-only Gemmi scaling
  -> final work-set fit
  -> information spectrum
  -> freeze model
  -> manual one-shot free evaluation
```

No production FFT optimization, information-spectrum solve, or accelerator test is intended for a login node.

## Tests

```bash
make check
```

CI runs Python 3.11-3.13 with `uv`, `ruff`, and `pytest`. Tests are divided into unit, lightweight integration, GPU, and cluster categories. GPU tests are run through `slurm/40_gpu_tests.sbatch`; expensive end-to-end tests remain cluster-only.

## Transfer

```bash
scripts/package_transfer.sh /path/to/crystal-field-inference-transfer.tar.gz
```

The archive excludes data, run products, caches and local environments, and includes the source, configs, paper, tests, SLURM jobs and environment bootstrap.

## Repository layout

```text
configs/       dataset and prior configurations
src/           scientific code
slurm/         SLURM jobs
scripts/       environment, transfer and submission helpers
tests/         unit, integration, GPU and cluster tests
docs/          design, HPC and execution notes
paper/         manuscript source
containers/    optional Apptainer definition
```

## 6O2H references

- Meisburger SP, Case DA, Ando N. Diffuse X-ray scattering from correlated motions in a protein crystal. Nature Communications (2020). DOI: 10.1038/s41467-020-14933-6.
- Wojdyr M. GEMMI: A library for structural biology. JOSS (2022). DOI: 10.21105/joss.04200.
- Greisman JB, Dalton KM, Hekstra DR. reciprocalspaceship: a Python library for crystallographic data analysis. J. Appl. Cryst. (2021). DOI: 10.1107/S160057672100755X.

## Generic PDB workflow

For any four-character PDB entry with deposited X-ray structure factors:

```bash
uv run fieldrefine init-pdb PDBID
uv run cfi inspect configs/pdbid/default.yaml
```

Then submit with `scripts/submit.sh CONFIG.yaml`. Entries without deposited structure factors require a user-supplied MTZ/CIF reflection file.

## Generic input contract

The core pipeline accepts any four-character PDB ID for which a compatible X-ray structure-factor file is available. `fieldrefine init-pdb PDBID` downloads the current RCSB coordinate mmCIF and deposited structure factors, discovers reflection columns, and writes a dataset-specific YAML. RCSB entries without deposited structure factors must use a user-supplied MTZ or reflection CIF. The field and inference code does not contain a hard-coded PDB ID.

The generated config uses deposited free flags when present. When no free flags are deposited, the initializer falls back to deterministic hash holdout and the report must be interpreted as a held-out test statistic, not a deposited crystallographic R-free value.

